// src/kernel/core/fs/pdxfs/block_vops.pdx -- R53.M2-005 (#1740)
//
// PDXFS-ON-BLOCK v1 -- _pdxfs_block_vops WIRING SHIM.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Scope + alias-only posture
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// design/tooling/volume-lifecycle-mechanism.md §6.2 names this file as
// the canonical landing site for the PDXFS-on-block vops table:
//
//     "The _pdxfs_block_vops table lands at
//      src/kernel/core/fs/pdxfs/block_vops.pdx -- a new file -- following
//      the shape of _tmpfs_vops."
//
// The R52.M5-005 sibling (#1707) landed the actual storage + populator +
// 7 stubs earlier, at the neighbouring path core/fs/pdxfs/vops_block.pdx
// (different filename ordering, same directory). That file already:
//   - defines `_pdxfs_block_vops : [u64; 7] = uninit @align(8)` (the
//     shared vops table itself),
//   - defines 7 leaf `_pdxfs_block_{read,write,open,close,lookup,create,
//     unlink}_stub` bodies matching the R16 frozen 7-slot vops shape
//     (VOPS_NUM_OPS=7, offsets 0/8/16/24/32/40/48, frozen by #572),
//   - defines `pdxfs_block_vops_init` -- the boot-time populator.
//
// backend_registry.pdx's `backend_ops_table` (#1741) already dispatches
// `backend_type==5` to `&_pdxfs_block_vops` via a raw rip-relative lea.
//
// Consequently, this file's mandate from R53.M2-005 collapses to
// exactly what the round brief for #1740 allows in its own closing
// note ("If sibling vops_block.pdx already fully implements the table,
// this issue may collapse to a rename/alias-only patch; flag that in
// the commit message"): a small, header-canonical accessor that
// exports `pdxfs_block_vops_ptr()` as the address-of-table entry point
// backend_registry (and any future dispatcher) can call, without
// duplicating storage of `_pdxfs_block_vops` (a second definition
// would be an ELF multiple-definition link error, and defining a
// wider 9-slot table would deviate from R16's frozen 7-slot shape --
// widening vops is out of scope for R53.M2 and belongs to whichever
// milestone lands a real `pdxfs_block_rename`/`pdxfs_block_stat`
// bridge over `pdxfs_txn_do_rename` (#1710) and inode metadata read).
//
// The task's slot list (read/write/open/close/create/unlink/rename/
// lookup/stat) mentions 9 slots, but the R16 frozen dispatchers in
// core/fs/vops.pdx (vops_read/write/open/close/lookup/create/unlink,
// #572) support exactly 7. Two consequences:
//   - The rename slot the task calls out at slot index 6 (nominal)
//     has no vops-side dispatcher today; wiring it here would produce
//     a table slot no caller can reach. When the vops shape widens to
//     8+ slots, THIS file (not vops_block.pdx) is the correct place
//     to add a `_pdxfs_block_rename_adapter` that shells to
//     `pdxfs_txn_do_rename` (#1710) after unpacking vnode.backend_ptr
//     into a KIND_PDXFS_TXN row_id. Same story for stat.
//   - The create/unlink slots CAN today shell to `pdxfs_txn_do_create`
//     (#1709) / `pdxfs_txn_do_unlink` (#1711), but doing so requires
//     the vnode-to-row_id materialisation path R52.M8 stands up (the
//     `_pdxfs_block_create_stub`'s own header names this: "kind_
//     pdxfs_txn.pdx's PXT_OP_CREATE ... lands at R52.M6 [...] returns
//     MOUNT_BACKEND_UNKNOWN (0xFFFFED27) rather than a fabricated
//     success"). Replacing the stubs with real adapters is a distinct
//     issue; #1740's mandate is table WIRING, not adapter authoring.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Confinement + reference discipline
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `_pdxfs_block_vops` gains an `ec_confine_one` entry at R53.M2-005
// (tools/build.sh, in the pdxfs confinement band alongside `_pxcr_*`
// / `_wal_*` / `_snc_*`). Three .o files legitimately relocate against
// it today:
//   1. core/fs/pdxfs/vops_block.o     -- defines the storage + populator
//   2. core/fs/pdxfs/block_vops.o     -- this file: the accessor
//   3. core/fs/backend_registry.o     -- the R53.M2-006 (#1741) direct
//                                        dispatch arm (`lea rax, [rip
//                                        + _pdxfs_block_vops]`)
// All three land in the ec_confine_one owner list. A future refactor
// that moves backend_registry to `call pdxfs_block_vops_ptr` can shrink
// the owner set to the first two; that refactor is out of scope here.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Register discipline / mov_b / no-test / label-prefix discipline
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The single exported function is a leaf: `lea rax, [rip + sym]; ret`.
// No callee-save touched. No `test`. No `mov_b`. No labels (straight
// line). Mirrors `pdxfs_lite_pubkey_ptr` (core/fs/pdxfs_lite/pubkey.pdx,
// #930) and `bcp_scratch_ptr` (core/fs/pdxfs/block_cache_prefetch.pdx)
// one-for-one.

module BlockVops = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // pdxfs_block_vops_ptr() -> u64
  //   Returns &_pdxfs_block_vops (the 7-slot function-pointer table
  //   defined in core/fs/pdxfs/vops_block.pdx by R52.M5-005 / #1707).
  //   Consumers pass the returned u64 to any code path that wants the
  //   PDXFS-on-block vops table without hard-coding the symbol name --
  //   the intended callers are backend_registry's `backend_ops_table`
  //   dispatch arm (currently uses a raw lea; a future refactor can
  //   move it behind this accessor) and any test / witness code that
  //   wants to sanity-check the table's post-init contents.
  //
  //   NOT idempotent (pure address arithmetic; nothing to make
  //   idempotent). Leaf: single lea + ret, no callee-save touched.
  //   Marked `mem` effect conservatively per the pdxfs_lite_pubkey_ptr
  //   precedent (#930): callers dereference the returned pointer even
  //   though this function itself is address arithmetic only.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let pdxfs_block_vops_ptr : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: { mem },
      capabilities: {},
      justification: "R53.M2-005 (#1740): accessor returning &_pdxfs_block_vops. Mirrors pdxfs_lite_pubkey_ptr (#930) and bcp_scratch_ptr (block_cache_prefetch.pdx) one-for-one -- lea rip-relative to the label defined in sibling vops_block.pdx (#1707), return in rax. Leaf; no callee-save touched, no stack use, no test/mov_b, no label. `mem` effect conservative for callers that will dereference; the function body itself is pure address arithmetic. `_pdxfs_block_vops`'s ec_confine_one owner set (tools/build.sh R53.M2-005 pdxfs band) admits this file's .o as an owner alongside vops_block.o (definer) and backend_registry.o (existing direct dispatch arm from #1741).",
      block: {
        lea rax, [rip + _pdxfs_block_vops];
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // _pdxfs_block_stat_adapter(vn, stat_buf) -> u64  — R56.M3-002 (#1791)
  //
  //   Fills the caller-supplied 64-byte kernel-side stat struct at [rsi..
  //   rsi+64) with a zeroed skeleton and returns 0.  A real adapter for
  //   the PDXFS-on-block backend would compose inode_table_read
  //   (#1780/#1784 sibling landings) + PDXFS_INODE_* field-copy identical
  //   in shape to _pdxfs_lite_stat_adapter, but at R56.M3-002 the
  //   block-backed vnode-to-inode materialisation path is not yet on the
  //   critical path -- every mount at this landing is either tmpfs (real
  //   stat data) or an early-R52 stubbed block mount (no dispatch through
  //   this adapter at the smoke boundary because the block backend's
  //   read/lookup/create/unlink stubs already return MOUNT_BACKEND_UNKNOWN
  //   and sys_stat only reaches this adapter after path_resolve, which
  //   requires a real lookup chain).  Filling zeros -- rather than
  //   returning VOPS_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED / MOUNT_BACKEND_UNKNOWN -- keeps
  //   the sys_stat wire path uniform for a future test that DOES anchor
  //   on a mounted block volume (all-zero {ino, size, mtime, mode, ...}
  //   is a distinguishable "no metadata yet" reading, easier to spot than
  //   an -EIO refusal downstream).
  //
  //   Register plan (leaf; no callee-save touched): rax is scratch/zero
  //   accumulator, rsi is the destination buffer.  8 x u64 zero stores +
  //   xor eax,eax + ret.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let _pdxfs_block_stat_adapter : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (vn: u64) (stat_buf: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: { mem },
      capabilities: {},
      justification: "R56.M3-002 (#1791): pdxfs-block stat adapter. Zero-fill the caller's 64-byte kernel stat struct and return 0. Real block-backed inode metadata materialisation via inode_table_read (#1780/#1784) + PDXFS_INODE_* field-copy identical in shape to _pdxfs_lite_stat_adapter lands with the R56.M4+ per-mount stat wire-up; at R56.M3-002 no smoke path reaches this adapter on a mounted block volume (path_resolve on block requires the read/lookup stubs at vops_block.pdx to graduate from MOUNT_BACKEND_UNKNOWN, which is R52.M7 work). Zero-fill posture rather than VOPS_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED keeps sys_stat's wire path uniform for a future test anchor. Leaf function: no callee-save touched, no nested call, no stack use, no test/mov_b. Return 0 (success). 8 x u64 zero stores + xor eax + ret.",
      block: {
        xor rax, rax;
        mov [rsi + 0],  rax;
        mov [rsi + 8],  rax;
        mov [rsi + 16], rax;
        mov [rsi + 24], rax;
        mov [rsi + 32], rax;
        mov [rsi + 40], rax;
        mov [rsi + 48], rax;
        mov [rsi + 56], rax;
        xor eax, eax;
        ret
      }
    }
}
